Free will is a foundational concept in ethics, allowing for moral responsibility and accountability for one's actions.
Neuroscientific research indicates that the brain can generate spontaneous decisions without being fully determined by prior stimuli.
The capacity for self-reflection and moral reasoning is unique to humans and implies a level of autonomy not observed in other species.
In everyday life, people routinely make choices that contradict their previous behavior, showing the presence of independent decision-making.
Philosophical traditions across cultures have long recognized the existence of free will as a defining feature of human existence.